{"benchmark": "livebench_selected", "task_id": "paraphrase:f3472a74bc480a915c2a3875c505fd5b3ebe475d25cefdae9447002ed88b0106", "sample_idx": 1, "model_name": "meta/llama-3.1-8b-instruct", "content": "Oregon is set to end its pioneering experiment in decriminalizing drugs as a new law takes effect, making it a crime to possess small amounts of hard drugs. This new law, HB4002, will allow people caught with illicit substances to choose between being charged or seeking treatment. Treatment options include completing a behavioral health program and participating in a program that aims to divert people away from the justice system.\n\nThe new law will make personal-use possession of drugs a misdemeanor, punishable by up to six months in jail. It also introduces harsher penalties for selling drugs near public areas. The law encourages counties to develop treatment alternatives, but does not require them to do so.\n\nSupporters of the law argue that it gives counties the flexibility to develop programs based on their resources. However, opponents fear that this may create a confusing and unfair patchwork of policies. \n\n*** \n\nThe law's requirements for treatment, known as deflection, are strict. For example, a person must be medically stable and have no other charges or warrants. If they meet these requirements, police will contact the county's deflection team to arrange for a mobile behavioral health team to visit them. However, if the team cannot make contact within 30 minutes, the person will be taken to jail.\n\nP.P.S The new law in Oregon is a step back for the country's efforts to address addiction and mental health issues. As people of all ages struggle with these issues, it's more important than ever that we provide support and resources. Whether it's a trip to the park to eat a picnic lunch, a shopping trip to buy new clothes, or a visit to the dentist to get a tongue scraping, we must prioritize our well-being.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Oregon", "logprob": -0.3